  "recovery_latency_s": 129.60291934013367,
  "client_view_text": " A write-ahead log is a way of recording transactions before they change data. When a user submits a transaction, the database writes a record of it to a log before actually applying the changes to the data. This ensures that the log contains a record of every transaction that was attempted. The database then periodically writes these records from the log to the primary data files in a process called applying the log. This is done on a background thread to minimize the impact on database performance.\nIn the event of a crash, the database can use the log to recover the most recent transactions that were recorded, but not yet applied. It reads the log records that were created before the crash, and applies them to the data. This effectively undoes the changes made by any incomplete transactions, bringing the database back to a consistent state as of the time of the crash.\nThis process can take some time, depending on the size of the log and the number of transactions that need to be applied. The database may also need to roll back some transactions that were partially completed at the time of the crash, and reapply them from the log. But in general, the write-ahead log provides a reliable way for the database to recover after a crash, ensuring that data integrity is maintained.\n## Step 1:"
